    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but doesn't that democratization come at a cost though? It feels like when the message becomes the main point, the actual craftsmanship and the ability to evoke emotion through beauty gets sidelined. Like, if a piece is just a political statement, does it still really hold the same artistic value as something that can stand on its own without needing a manifesto to explain it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This is a crucial tension in modern art, often framed as the \"argument versus artifact\" debate. While strong political art claims value through its persuasive power and social commentary, the critique is that this can lead to \"preachy\" or un nuanced works that prioritize slogans over sophisticated execution. The enduring value of the \"unadorned\" piece often lies in its formal power and its ability to trigger a deeply personal, non-political emotional response, which is a completely different, but equally valid, metric for worth.",
      "source": "target",
